course: dictionary
lesson: 2591
title: The layers of a tooth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tooth is not one solid thing. It has layers, and each layer has its own job. Scientists use words like enamel, dentine and pulp to name them. You can use the context around a word to work out what it means.
explain: «Under the enamel is dentine, full of tiny tubes leading towards the pulp.» The word under tells you where dentine sits: below the enamel. The words full of tiny tubes and leading towards the pulp tell you it is a layer that sits between the outside and the middle of the tooth.
explain: Now read on: «...the soft middle with the nerve.» The pulp is the soft middle of the tooth, with a nerve inside it. If the outer layers wear thin, cold or heat can reach that nerve, and the tooth zings. Always check: does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: Dentine is the hard, yellowish layer that makes up most of a tooth.
fact: Enamel is the hard, white outer layer, and it sits on top of the dentine.
fact: The pulp is the soft middle of a tooth, with nerves and blood vessels inside it.
fact: When enamel wears away, the dentine can be exposed, and teeth may feel sensitive to cold.
fact: Elephant tusks are mostly dentine, which is also called ivory.
exercise: «Under the enamel is dentine, full of tiny tubes leading towards the pulp.» Where is dentine found? | On top of the enamel | Outside the gum | *Under the enamel | In the middle, where the pulp is
exercise: Which part of a tooth is the soft middle with the nerve? | Enamel | Dentine | Gum | *Pulp
exercise: What colour is dentine? | Blue | *Yellowish | White | Black
test: When enamel wears away, which layer can be exposed? | Pulp | Gum | Root | *Dentine
test: Elephant tusks are mostly made of which tooth material? | Pulp | *Dentine | Enamel | Gum
exam: When enamel wears thin and cold reaches a tooth, which layer is most likely exposed, and why? | Gum only, because the tooth has no inner layers | Pulp, because it is the hard outer layer | *Dentine, because it lies under the enamel and has tiny tubes leading towards the pulp | Enamel, because it is soft and yellow
